{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LctqV6P9qVciGqUShCbWKP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Prototype: Tesseract OCR Image Processor\n2\t\n3\t## Problem\n4\tManually running `tesseract` per image is tedious for bulk scans. Users also want to visually verify OCR results by seeing highlighted words overlaid on the original image, which requires structured output (hOCR) rather than plain text.\n5\t\n6\t## Approach\n7\t- `ocr.sh <input_dir> <output_dir>` with optional `--lang` flag (default: `eng`)\n8\t- Use `find` to locate `.png`, `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.tiff`, `.tif` in input dir\n9\t- For each image, run `tesseract <img> <output_base> txt hocr pdf` — produces `.txt`, `.hocr`, `.pdf`\n10\t- Mirror input subdirectory structure in output dir\n11\t- Log successes and failures to `<output_dir>/ocr.log`\n12\t- The `.hocr` file contains word bounding boxes; a simple `hocr-viewer.html` (single file) can render image + highlight overlay in-browser\n13\t\n14\t## Data / State\n15\t- Input: image files (read-only)\n16\t- Output: `.txt`, `.hocr`, `.pdf` per image + `ocr.log`\n17\t- No persistent state between runs; re-running overwrites output for the same filename\n18\t\n19\t## Open Questions\n20\t- `tesseract` PDF output embeds invisible text over the image — does that count as \"highlight words from the image\"? If the user wants visible highlights, an hOCR HTML viewer is the right path.\n21\t- Should existing output files be skipped (resume mode) or always overwritten? Skipping would make re-runs faster for large batches.\n22\t- Multi-page TIFFs: `tesseract` handles them natively, but output naming differs — verify behavior.\n23\t\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a9a0c21-250b-47df-bfb8-035bd6b27178","uuid":"8b615cf3-2535-4fcb-80b1-9ef969080aa7","timestamp":"2026-04-23T04:16:43.610Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/sysoper/workspace/WhileISleep/batch-output/tesseract-ocr-image-processor/prototype.md","content":"# Prototype: Tesseract OCR Image Processor\n\n## Problem\nManually running `tesseract` per image is tedious for bulk scans. Users also want to visually verify OCR results by seeing highlighted words overlaid on the original image, which requires structured output (hOCR) rather than plain text.\n\n## Approach\n- `ocr.sh <input_dir> <output_dir>` with optional `--lang` flag (default: `eng`)\n- Use `find` to locate `.png`, `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.tiff`, `.tif` in input dir\n- For each image, run `tesseract <img> <output_base> txt hocr pdf` — produces `.txt`, `.hocr`, `.pdf`\n- Mirror input subdirectory structure in output dir\n- Log successes and failures to `<output_dir>/ocr.log`\n- The `.hocr` file contains word bounding boxes; a simple `hocr-viewer.html` (single file) can render image + highlight overlay in-browser\n\n## Data / State\n- Input: image files (read-only)\n- Output: `.txt`, `.hocr`, `.pdf` per image + `ocr.log`\n- No persistent state between runs; re-running overwrites output for the same filename\n\n## Open Questions\n- `tesseract` PDF output embeds invisible text over the image — does that count as \"highlight words from the image\"? If the user wants visible highlights, an hOCR HTML viewer is the right path.\n- Should existing output files be skipped (resume mode) or always overwritten? Skipping would make re-runs faster for large batches.\n- Multi-page TIFFs: `tesseract` handles them natively, but output naming differs — verify behavior.\n\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DYyjahQwhJKWHeT6RtQH7h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what needs to be built. Let me check the CLAUDE.md more carefully - the Open Questions section says:\n- Should produce both `.txt` and `.hocr` \n- Response: pdf approach (for the second question about which format enables \"highlight words on original image\")\n\nWait, re-reading the CLAUDE.md:\n- \"Should the script produce `.hocr` + `.txt` separately, or a single searchable PDF per image? Assume both: `.txt` for quick reading, `.hocr` for overlay capability.\"\n\n\n- The second question asks which format enables highlighting words on the original image — hOCR with a viewer allows that, while PDF embeds text invisibly. The user's response indicates they want the PDF approach.\n\nLooking at prototype.md, I see the command runs tesseract with multiple output formats at once: txt, hocr, and pdf all get generated from a single call.\n\nThere's a tension here — the CLAUDE.md response says \"pdf approach\" but the prototype explicitly produces all three formats. Since the first question answer was \"both\" (txt for quick reading, hocr for overlay), and the prototype calls for all three outputs, I think the safest move is to generate all three formats as specified. The \"pdf approach\" response probably just means to include PDF output alongside the others, not to exclude hOCR.\n\nSo my plan is to build the main OCR script, a single-file HTML viewer for rendering hOCR with highlighting, a Dockerfile using tesseract, and a dockerignore file.\n\nFor the Dockerfile, I'll use a base image with tesseract already installed, include some test images (or generate them with ImageMagick during the build), and run the script to verify it works. Writing the main bash script now...\n\nStill writing script... Setting up the log file and initializing counters for tracking successful, failed, and skipped files, then starting to iterate through images in the input directory to process them based on their file extensions.\n\nNow I'm constructing the output paths for each image, creating necessary directories, and running tesseract to extract text, hOCR, and PDF outputs while logging the results and updating the success/failure counters.\n\nI'm realizing there's a problem with using `(( FAILURE++ ))` and `(( SUCCESS++ ))` when `set -e` is enabled, since incrementing from zero returns a false exit code that would cause the script to exit prematurely. I need to switch to arithmetic assignment like `FAILURE=$((FAILURE + 1))` instead. For the Dockerfile, I'll start with a Debian base image that has tesseract installed, generate some test images, and run the script to verify it works.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the output formats—the prototype mentions producing txt, hocr, and pdf files, and I should create a simple HTML viewer that can display the image with hocr highlighting overlaid on top of it.\n\nFor the Dockerfile, I'll use debian:12-slim as the base and install tesseract for OCR along with ImageMagick to generate test images during the build.\n\nSetting up the Dockerfile... I'm realizing that tesseract automatically appends the appropriate file extensions based on the output format specified, so when I run it with a base output name like `/output/test1`, it creates separate files for each format—the `.txt`, `.hocr`, and `.pdf` files all get generated with that base name. I need to verify the exact command syntax for tesseract 4+ to make sure I'm specifying the output formats correctly.\n\nI'm also thinking about the font availability in the slim Debian image—DejaVu Sans might not be included by default, so I should add the `fonts-dejavu-core` package to the apt install to ensure the text rendering works properly.\n\nFor creating the test image, I'll use ImageMagick's `-annotate` command with a simpler approach that's more reliable than text drawing operations, and it should work with whatever fonts are available after installation.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a bash arithmetic issue with the success counter - using `(( SUCCESS++ ))` will fail when SUCCESS is 0 since the expression evaluates to the old value. I'll use `SUCCESS=$((SUCCESS + 1))` instead to safely increment the counters.\n\nFor the hOCR viewer, I need to build an HTML page that accepts image and hOCR file uploads, parses the XML to extract word bounding boxes from the `bbox` attribute in the title field, and then overlay colored rectangles on the image at the correct coordinates, accounting for any scaling.\n\nBuilding the HTML interface... Writing the file handling logic... Writing the render function...\n\nNow I'm thinking about the ImageMagick font issue in the Debian slim image—I should install the core DejaVu fonts to ensure text rendering works properly.\n\nFor Tesseract, Debian 12 should have version 5.x available through the standard repos, which should work well for OCR. I'm also considering a few potential compatibility issues: the find command syntax for locating image files, the bash parameter expansion for converting extensions to lowercase, and how Tesseract names its output files. I'm leaning toward using a more portable find syntax with explicit file extension matching rather than regex patterns.\n\nWriting the OCR script... Writing the OCR processing loop... I'm restructuring how I capture the exit code from tesseract to make sure I'm getting the right value—using a variable assignment with the `||` operator so I can reliably check whether the command succeeded or failed, regardless of the `set -e` setting.\n\nFor the Dockerfile, I'm planning to generate a test image using ImageMagick with a system font to create something tesseract can actually process.\n\nI'll also create a second test image in a subdirectory to verify the script handles nested directory structures correctly. One thing I need to watch out for is ImageMagick's security policy in Debian 12 — it's pretty restrictive by default, but basic PNG generation from text should work fine.\n\nFor the CMD instruction, I'm setting up a comprehensive test that runs the OCR script and then displays all the output files to verify everything worked correctly. After that, I should update the README with setup instructions and a to-do list, then write out all the necessary files.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the tesseract output format—when I run it with the txt, hocr, and pdf flags, it generates three separate files with those extensions, which should work correctly for recent tesseract versions. I'll start writing all the source files now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":975,"cache_read_input_tokens":18202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":975},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a9a0c21-250b-47df-bfb8-035bd6b27178","uuid":"9eda6bd5-7d17-4bf5-8358-56a3c0d30729"}
